{"id": 725, "stem": "<div class=\"relative w-full min-w-0 max-w-full self-stretch\" id=\"question-text\"><span><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><mark class=\"bg-transparent dark:text-neutral-100\" data-markjs=\"true\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">A</mark> 4-year-old boy is brought to the office due to skin lesions that appeared on his chin 4 days ago.\u00a0 Temperature is 36.1 C (97 F).\u00a0 The patient appears well.\u00a0 Mildly tender perioral papules and pustules are noted on examination, some of which have broken down and are covered with an amber-colored crusting.\u00a0 The remainder of the examination is unremarkable.\u00a0 A specimen is collected from the wound.\u00a0 Gram stain shows gram-positive cocci in chains.\u00a0 Cultures are pending.\u00a0 Which of the following symptoms would be most likely to develop as a potential complication of this patient's infection?\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b</p></span></div>", "options": [{"label": "A", "text": "Ascending weakness"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Diffuse erythema and desquamation"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Facial puffiness and dark urine"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Joint pain and eye redness"}, {"label": "E", "text": "Unilateral facial drooping"}], "correctAnswer": "C", "explanation": "<div class=\"relative w-full min-w-0 max-w-full self-stretch\" id=\"question-explanation\"><span class=\"\"><div id=\"div_T89736\" style=\"overflow-x: auto; font-size: 1rem;\"><div class=\"group/triggerable\" data-is-open=\"false\"><div class=\"group/draggable\" data-is-draggable=\"true\" data-is-dragging=\"false\"><div class=\"group/resizable relative\" data-is-maximized=\"false\" data-is-resizable=\"true\" data-is-resizing=\"false\"><div class=\"relative max-w-full\"><div aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"pointer-events-none absolute left-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-8 z-10 transition-opacity duration-200 bg-gradient-to-r from-white dark:from-nbme-primary-dark-800 warm:from-egg-sour-50 to-transparent opacity-0\"></div><div aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"pointer-events-none absolute right-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-8 z-10 transition-opacity duration-200 bg-gradient-to-l from-white dark:from-nbme-primary-dark-800 warm:from-egg-sour-50 to-transparent opacity-0\"></div><div class=\"max-w-full overflow-x-auto [&amp;_td]:min-w-[8rem] [&amp;_th]:min-w-[8rem]\"><table class=\"border bg-transparent dark:text-white border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white hover:bg-transparent warm:hover:bg-transparent dark:hover:bg-transparent cursor-default mt-2 mb-3\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><tbody style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 100%; font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><mark class=\"bg-transparent dark:text-neutral-50\" data-markjs=\"true\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">I</mark>mpetigo</strong></p></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"width: 29.98%; font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Type</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"width: 35%; font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Nonbullous</strong> (more common)</p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"width: 35%; font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Bullous</strong></p></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"width: 29.98%; font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Microbiology</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"width: 35%; font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><em style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Staphylococcus aureus</em> (most common)</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Group A <em style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Streptococcus</em></li></ul></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"width: 35%; font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><em style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">S aureus</em></li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"width: 29.98%; font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Mechanism</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"width: 35%; font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Skin disruption</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Invasion &amp; general inflammatory response</li></ul></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"width: 35%; font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Intact skin</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Exfoliative toxin destroys cell adhesion molecules (eg, desmoglein)</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"width: 29.98%; font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Clinical features</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"width: 35%; font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Papules \u2192 pustules \u2192 honey-colored crusts</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Negative Nikolsky sign*</li></ul></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"width: 35%; font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Vesicles \u2192 flaccid bullae \u2192 thin, brown crusts</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Positive Nikolsky sign*</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"width: 29.98%; font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Complications</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 70%; font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Group A <em style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Streptococcus</em>: poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><em style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">S aureus</em>: staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 100%; font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">*In conditions with impaired adherence between epidermal layers, rubbing gently (mechanical pressure) against the skin causes the upper &amp; lower epidermal layers to separate, creating a blister (positive Nikolsky sign).</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></div></div></div><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">This child has <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">impetigo</strong>, a superficial bacterial infection that most commonly develops on the face or extremities in children.\u00a0 Patients usually have mildly itchy or painful erythematous papules and pustules that later erupt, forming a <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-uetjp6v74kbahi1gm06sgyn4\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-uetjp6v74kbahi1gm06sgyn4\">honey-colored crust</a>.\u00a0 The most common pathogen is <em style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Staphylococcus aureus</em>, followed by <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">group A</strong> <em style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Streptococcus</strong></em> (GAS), specifically <em style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.\u00a0 In this patient, microscopy of the exudate shows <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">gram-positive cocci in chains</strong>, indicating a streptococcal infection.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">Localized impetigo is treated with topical antibiotics, and most patients have no long-term sequelae.\u00a0 However, a small number of patients with impetigo due to GAS develop <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis</strong> (PSGN) several weeks after the initial infection.\u00a0 Kidney damage is caused by immune complex deposition and subsequent complement activation, precipitating an inflammatory response that <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-cwr9vdwgcku4o4d5c2xi6v0i\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-cwr9vdwgcku4o4d5c2xi6v0i\">damages the glomerular basement membrane</a>.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">Presentations of PSGN range from asymptomatic, microscopic hematuria to overt <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">nephritic syndrome</strong>; pyuria and nephritic-range (&lt;3.5 g/day) proteinuria are common findings.\u00a0 In addition to tea- or <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">cola-colored urine</strong>, symptomatic patients may develop periorbital/<strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">facial swelling</strong>, generalized edema, and hypertension.\u00a0 Laboratory evaluation typically shows elevated creatinine and decreased C3 levels.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice A)</strong>\u00a0 Ascending weakness is a classic feature of Guillain-Barr\u00e9 syndrome, which may be preceded by an upper respiratory or gastrointestinal (eg, <em style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Campylobacter jejuni</em>) tract infection.\u00a0 A GAS infection is not associated with this condition.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice B)</strong>\u00a0 Diffuse erythema with a sandpaper-like quality and subsequent desquamation occurs in scarlet fever, which is a delayed-type skin reaction to a GAS exotoxin.\u00a0 This condition typically develops within 2 days of the initial infection and is usually associated with fever and GAS pharyngitis, findings inconsistent with this patient.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice D)</strong>\u00a0 Joint pain and eye redness (due to conjunctivitis or anterior uveitis) may be seen in reactive arthritis, which typically occurs following a diarrheal (eg, <em style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Salmonella</em>) or genitourinary tract (eg, <em style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Chlamydia</em>) infection, not impetigo.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice E)</strong>\u00a0 Unilateral facial drooping due to a peripheral <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-a3earcf3c2sthbvw7zzlnqoa\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-a3earcf3c2sthbvw7zzlnqoa\">facial nerve palsy</a> is most commonly idiopathic (ie, Bell palsy) or associated with neuroborreliosis or herpes simplex virus infection.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Educational objective:</strong>Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis is a potential complication of group A streptococcal infection (eg, impetigo, pharyngitis).\u00a0 Symptoms include proteinuria (often associated with periorbital/facial swelling or generalized edema) and hematuria.\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b</p></span></div>", "educationalObjective": "Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis is a potential complication of group A streptococcal infection (eg, impetigo, pharyngitis).  Symptoms include proteinuria (often associated with periorbital/facial swelling or generalized edema) and hematuria.\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b